<section class="vehicle-studies" aria-labelledby="hardware-title" id="flight-hardware">
  <div class="studies-heading"><p>Flight hardware / Concept studies</p><h2 id="hardware-title">What makes the handoff?</h2></div>
  <div class="studies-grid">
    <figure class="carrier-study"><div><img src="/vehicles/carrier-loaded.webp" alt="Graphite carrier with inward-canted twin tails and a copper capsule on its dorsal saddle" width="1440" height="760" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></div>
      <figcaption><small>01 / Earth departure</small><h3>The carrier</h3><p>The aircraft brings the cargo up to the tether. Only the capsule goes on; the carrier returns to Earth.</p></figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div><img src="/vehicles/earth-cargo.webp" alt="Copper-wrapped Earth cargo bus with an exposed capture fitting" width="704" height="704" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></div>
      <figcaption><small>02 / Earth cargo</small><h3>The cargo bus</h3><p>The grapple closes around the fitting above the insulated cargo bay, clear of the aircraft.</p></figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div><img src="/vehicles/lunar-cargo.webp" alt="Pale lunar cargo canister with copper bands and a compact service end" width="704" height="704" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></div>
      <figcaption><small>03 / Lunar cargo</small><h3>The lunar canister</h3><p>The canister rides a reusable sled through the coils. The sled brakes; the cargo coasts toward the lunavator.</p></figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</section>
